      },
      "worked": "An upper semi-circle has the form $y=\\sqrt{r^2-(x-h)^2}$, with $r>0$. The coefficient of $x^2$ under the square root is $-1$, so $a=-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\sqrt{-x^2+bx+c}\\\\y&=\\sqrt{\\frac{b^2}{4}+c-\\left(x-\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe radius squared is $\\frac{b^2}{4}+c$, which must be positive. Hence $a=-1$ and $b^2+4c>0$.\n\nWith $a=-1$, this is equivalent to requiring the discriminant $b^2-4ac$ to be positive.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
